SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Attention Trading Partners: Training for New 
 MaineCare Partner Portal
DESCRIPTION:Maine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Ma
 ine.gov\nDHHS\n→\nOffice of MaineCare Services (OMS)\n→\nProvider Reso
 urces\n→\n	 \n			Attention Trading Partners: Training for New MaineCare 
 Partner Portal\nAttention Trading Partners: Training for New MaineCare Par
 tner Portal\nAug 12\, 2026\nWe have\npreviously shared\nthat a refreshed a
 nd upgraded MIHMS\, with a new “MaineCare Partner Portal\,” is coming 
 in October 2026. This will replace the current HealthPAS Online Portal. We
  are now inviting trading partners to register for training starting Septe
 mber 8\, 2026 to help you prepare for the transition to the new portal.\nG
 ainwell Technologies will be leading live virtual instructor-led training 
 sessions. These courses will provide the knowledge and skills needed to na
 vigate the new portal\, complete daily business functions\, and use new po
 rtal features. Training demonstrations\, materials\, and supporting resour
 ces will help participants prepare for a successful transition.\nSteps you
  need to take to attend training\nReview the available training topics lis
 ted below and identify the sessions that align with your role and responsi
 bilities.\nUse the LMS registration (instructions below) to create an acco
 unt\, access the training catalog\, and enroll in applicable sessions.\nCo
 ntact Gainwell (instructions below) if you need assistance.\nRecorded sess
 ions and other training materials will be available in the LMS after train
 ing sessions for ongoing reference.\nMaineCare Partner Portal Training Ses
 sions\nCourse Name\nDescription\nME - TPT - Migrated Trading Partner Log I
 n and Account Maintenance Training (ILC)\nLearn how to complete first-time
  login activities\, configure multi-factor authentication (MFA)\, manage u
 ser accounts and permissions\, perform account maintenance activities\, an
 d understand electronic data interchange (EDI) certification requirements 
 and status monitoring. This course is only for Admin (R7) users.\nME - TPT
  - Trading Partner Registration and Account Maintenance Training (ILC)\nht
 tps://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/attention-tradin
 g-partners-training-new-mainecare-partner-portal-2026-08-12
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/attention-
 trading-partners-training-new-mainecare-partner-portal-2026-08-12
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:3002c865eb42f8a9@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260812T024135Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine CDC: Childhood Vaccine Guidance Remains
  Unchanged Following Federal Action
DESCRIPTION:Aug 11\, 2026\nMaine will continue to follow the guidance of t
 he American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) on childhood vaccine recommendatio
 ns\, despite President Donald Trump’s executive order on childhood vacci
 nations\, per the Maine Department of Health and Human Services’ Center 
 for Disease Control and Prevention (Maine CDC).\n“President Trump and Se
 cretary Kennedy are playing politics with the lives of our nation’s chil
 dren. Vaccines are safe and effective and save lives\,”\nsaid Governor J
 anet Mills.\n“Maine will continue to follow the science and the facts\, 
 and our approach to vaccines remains unchanged by yesterday’s executive 
 order.”\n“Vaccines are our most effective defense against vaccine-prev
 entable illnesses\,”\nsaid Dr. Puthiery Va\, Director of the Maine CDC.\
 n“In Maine\, vaccines remain available at no cost to eligible children t
 hrough the Maine CDC’s Immunization Program. Maine’s school and child 
 care immunization requirements remain unchanged by federal actions to date
 .”\nAs the AAP has noted in their 8/10\nstatement\nin response to this e
 xecutive order:\n“Vaccine recommendations from the American Academy of P
 ediatrics are tailored to the health of children living in the United Stat
 es and are based on decades of research and systematic tracking of real-wo
 rld experiences. Pediatricians recommend children receive certain vaccines
  at specific ages based on when children's immune systems will respond bes
 t and when they are most vulnerable to certain diseases. Sometimes this me
 ans children will receive a single shot that protects against multiple dis
 eases\, or they may receive multiple vaccines at one visit.”\nBeginning 
 in 2021\, Maine removed non-medical exemptions from the school and child c
 are immunization requirements pursuant\nPublic Law 2019\, Ch. 154\n. That 
 decision was upheld by a vote of 72.81 percent of Maine voters at the ball
 ot box in 2020.\nhttps://www.maine.gov/dhhs/news/maine-cdc-childhood-vacci
 ne-guidance-remains-unchanged-following-federal-action-2026-08-11
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/news/maine-cdc-childhood-vaccine-guidance-r
 emains-unchanged-following-federal-action-2026-08-11
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2382561993b810e0@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260811T191729Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] USDA Distributes Oral Rabies Vaccine for Wild
 life in Northern Maine
DESCRIPTION:Aug 11\, 2026\nIn cooperation with the Maine Department of Hea
 lth and Human Services’ Center for Disease Control and Prevention (Maine
  CDC)\, officials from U.S. Department of Agriculture’s (USDA) Wildlife 
 Services will distribute oral rabies vaccine (ORV) baits this week. USDA W
 ildlife Services will distribute nearly 599\,000 ORV baits in Aroostook Co
 unty from August 12 through August 24.\nThe fall ORV bait distribution fol
 lows this past spring’s distribution and is in response to continued rab
 id raccoon detections in Aroostook County. The USDA’s Wildlife Services 
 spreads ORV baits to reduce the spread of the raccoon rabies virus in Main
 e. The baits help limit the potential for human and domestic animal exposu
 res to the rabies virus through contact with infected wild animals.\nCrews
  will spread the bait by air in rural areas and by vehicle in more populat
 ed areas. Each bait is a fishmeal-coated cube or sachet about one to two i
 nches in size. Although humans and pets cannot get rabies from contact wit
 h the bait\, they should not touch or move them.\nStudies show that ORV ba
 its are safe in many species of animals\, including domestic dogs and cats
 . While dogs may get upset stomachs if they eat a lot of bait\, there are 
 no known long-term health risks. If a person has skin contact with a bait 
 cube or sachet\, rinse the affected area with warm water and soap. For con
 cerns about a person or pet touching or eating a bait\, call the Maine CDC
 ’s 24-hour line at 800-821-5821.\nSince 2003\, the USDA’s Wildlife Ser
 vices has worked to halt the spread of raccoon rabies in northern Maine. T
 hey also collaborate with Canadian officials in New Brunswick and Quebec t
 o reduce the presence of rabies across Maine and Canada. The ORV bait dist
 ribution program in Maine is part of a larger effort by the USDA’s Wild
 life Services to prevent the westward spread of raccoon rabies. This effor
 t creates a barrier along the Appalachian Mountains from the Canadian bord
 er to Alabama.\nRabies is a virus that infec\nhttps://www.maine.gov/dhhs/n
 ews/usda-distributes-oral-rabies-vaccine-wildlife-northern-maine-2026-08-1
 1
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/news/usda-distributes-oral-rabies-vaccine-w
 ildlife-northern-maine-2026-08-11
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:c5e9fc0a690bb4d0@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260811T191718Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260804
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] 8/4/2026: Maine eCourts Scheduled to be Imple
 mented in  Cumberland County Courts in September
DESCRIPTION:Home\nSkip to top navigation\nSkip to side navigation\nSkip to
  content\nSkip to footer\nMaine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp
 \n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nCOURT ALERTS IN EFFECT.\nSee Active Alerts\nHome\n
 →\nNews & Announcements\nMaine eCourts Scheduled to be Implemented in  C
 umberland County Courts in September\nDate:\n8/4/2026\nThe Maine Judicial 
 Branch announced today that the schedule for transitioning courts in Cumbe
 rland County (comprising Judicial Region 2) from paper filing to electroni
 c filing (eFiling) is scheduled for Monday\, September 28\, 2026. The next
  implementation includes all case types in the Cumberland County Unified C
 riminal Docket (UCD)\, Cumberland County Superior Court\, Portland Distric
 t Court\, and Bridgton District Court.\nAttorneys\, government agencies (e
 xcept law enforcement agencies)\, and parties who file more than six non-e
 mergency cases in a calendar year\, are required to eFile in courts that h
 ave implemented Maine eCourts. Self-represented parties are not required t
 o eFile and can continue to file documents as they always have\, in paper 
 at the courthouse\, by mail\, or by email if filing a protection from abus
 e or harassment case.\nAttorneys and other required eFilers should prepare
  in advance for the transition\, and take note of upcoming presentation an
 d trainings:\nPresentation for Cumberland County Legal Professionals and S
 takeholders\nThe Maine Judicial Branch will hold a virtual presentation by
  ZOOM on August 10th\, at noon to discuss the implementation of Maine eCou
 rts and electronic filing (eFiling) in Judicial Region 2\, Cumberland Coun
 ty Courts. Attorneys and other professional filers are encouraged to atten
 d.\nTopic: Maine eCourts in Region 2: Cumberland County\nDate & Time: Mond
 ay\, August 10\, 2026 at 12:00 p.m.\nPlease click the link below to join t
 he webinar:\nhttps://courts-maine-gov.zoom.us/j/94955753059\nPasscode: 993
 548\nThe webinar will be recorded and made available on the Judicial Branc
 h website on the Maine eCourts Training & Resources page followi\nhttps://
 www.courts.maine.gov/news/article.html?id=13357575
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/news/article.html?id=13357575
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f9c9e7e9f0c0df47@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260811T133947Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Response to Clean Air Act Section 176A Petiti
 on From New Hampshire
DESCRIPTION:The Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) is proposing to gran
 t a Clean Air Act (CAA or Act) petition submitted by the state of New Hamp
 shire on December 24\, 2025. The petition requests that the EPA remove the
  State of New Hampshire from the Ozone Transport Region (OTR) based on New
  Hampshire's continued attainment of the ozone National Ambient Air Qualit
 y Standards (NAAQS) and technical analyses demonstrating that the addition
 al control of emissions from the state will not significantly contribute t
 o ozone attainment in any area in the OTR. The OTR was established by the 
 1990 Clean Air Act Amendments and included the States of Connecticut\, Del
 aware\, Maine\, Maryland\, Massachusetts\, New Hampshire\, New Jersey\, Ne
 w York\, Pennsylvania\, Rhode Island\, Vermont\, the District of Columbia\
 , and portions of northern Virginia.\nhttps://www.federalregister.gov/docu
 ments/2026/08/11/2026-16331/response-to-clean-air-act-section-176a-petitio
 n-from-new-hampshire
URL:https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/11/2026-16331/respon
 se-to-clean-air-act-section-176a-petition-from-new-hampshire
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
